# Design System Rules

This document defines **UI and UX standards** for the website.

The goal is to achieve **exceptional usability and polish**.

All UI built by developers or AI agents must follow these rules.

This file exists to ensure:

- visual consistency
- intuitive workflows
- minimal friction
- high perceived quality

---

# Core Design Principles

The UI must always follow these principles.

## 1. Simplicity First

The UI should feel:

- lightweight
- fast
- focused

Avoid:

- complex navigation
- nested menus
- unnecessary settings

## 2. Minimal Cognitive Load

Users should never feel confused.

Always prioritize:

- clear labels
- simple controls
- obvious interactions

Every button must clearly communicate its purpose.

Bad example:

```
Action
```

Good example:

```
Capture Element
```

## 3. Visual Feedback

Every action must provide immediate feedback.

Examples:

- hover highlights
- loading states
- success notifications

Without feedback the UI will feel broken.

## 4. Speed Perception

The UI must **feel extremely fast**.

Rules:

- avoid blocking UI
- show loaders for operations >200ms
- keep animations short

Animation duration:

```
120ms–200ms
```

Never exceed:

```
300ms
```

---

# Component Architecture

## Self-Contained Components

Every component must be **self-contained** with all styles in the same folder.

Component structure:

```
ComponentName/
  ComponentName.tsx
  ComponentName.css
  index.ts
```

Rules:

- component imports its own CSS
- no global styles for component-specific layout
- styles use component tokens mapped to semantic tokens

---

# Design Tokens Architecture

This project uses a **3-layer design token system** for all CSS variables.

The goal is to ensure:
- consistent theming
- maintainable components
- scalable UI architecture
- safe refactoring

No component should reference raw colors or spacing values directly.

All styling must flow through the token layers defined below.

---

# Layer 1 — Primitive Tokens

Primitive tokens represent **raw design values**.

These should never describe usage or UI meaning.

Examples include:

- raw colors
- base spacing scale
- radii
- typography
- shadows

Naming format:

```
--gray-100, --gray-900, --blue-500, etc.
--space-1 through --space-8
--radius-1 through --radius-4
--font-sans, --font-mono
--shadow-sm, --shadow-md, --shadow-lg
```

Example:

```css
--gray-100: #f8fafc;
--space-2: 8px;
--radius-2: 8px;
```

Primitive tokens should **never be used directly inside component style declarations**.

---

# Layer 2 — Semantic Tokens

Semantic tokens describe **UI meaning**.

These map primitive tokens to real UI concepts.

Examples:

- text colors
- surfaces
- accents
- borders
- UI states

Naming format:

```
--color-{semantic-name}
```

Examples:

```
--color-text-primary
--color-text-secondary
--color-text-muted
--color-surface
--color-surface-alt
--color-panel
--color-border
--color-border-strong
--color-accent
--color-accent-hover
--color-accent-subtle
--color-overlay
--color-danger
```

Semantic tokens must map to primitives.

Example:

```css
--color-text-primary: var(--gray-900);
--color-border: var(--gray-200);
```

---

# Layer 3 — Component Tokens

Component tokens define styling for a specific component.

These are **aliases of semantic tokens**.

Naming format:

```
--{component}-{property}
```

Examples:

```
--card-bg
--card-border
--card-radius
--card-padding
--card-shadow-hover

--button-bg
--button-text
--button-radius
```

Component tokens must always reference semantic tokens or spacing tokens.

Example:

```css
.card {
  --card-bg: var(--color-panel);
  --card-border: var(--color-border);
  --card-radius: var(--radius-2);
  --card-padding: var(--space-3);
  --card-shadow-hover: var(--shadow-md);

  background: var(--card-bg);
  border: 1px solid var(--card-border);
  padding: var(--card-padding);
  border-radius: var(--card-radius);
}

.card:hover {
  box-shadow: var(--card-shadow-hover);
}
```

Component styles must only reference **component tokens**.

---

# No Runtime Themes

This project does not support runtime theme switching. All tokens are fixed in CSS.

---

# Color System

Use a minimal neutral palette.

Primary color:

```
#3b82f6
```

Neutral backgrounds:

```
#ffffff
#f8fafc
```

Borders:

```
#e5e7eb
```

Text:

```
#111827
```

Muted text:

```
#6b7280
```

---

# Typography

Use system fonts.

Preferred stack:

```
-apple-system
BlinkMacSystemFont
Segoe UI
Roboto
sans-serif
```

Font sizes:

```
Title: 16px
Body: 14px
Meta: 12px
```

---

# Iconography

Icons must be:

- simple
- recognizable
- consistent

Recommended icon library:

```
Lucide
Feather icons
```

Icons should be:

```
16px
```

---

# Spacing System

Use a consistent spacing scale.

```
4px
8px
12px
16px
24px
32px
```

Never use arbitrary spacing.

Always use spacing tokens.

---

# Radius Tokens

Radius tokens should follow the scale:

```
--radius-1: 4px
--radius-2: 8px
--radius-3: 12px
--radius-4: 16px
```

---

# Animation Rules

Animations should feel subtle.

Allowed animations:

```
hover elevation
fade in/out
button hover
```

Duration:

```
120ms–200ms
```

Avoid:

```
large transitions
complex motion
```

---

# Accessibility

UI must remain accessible.

Rules:

- maintain contrast ratio >4.5
- clickable areas >32px
- keyboard navigation supported

Buttons must always include:

```
aria-label
```

---

# Empty State Design

When content is empty show:

```
Empty illustration
Instruction text
Primary action button
```

Example:

```
No items yet

Add your first item to get started.

[ Add Item ]
```

Empty states are important for onboarding.

---

# Notifications

Small toast notifications should confirm actions.

Examples:

```
Item saved
Copied to clipboard
Item deleted
```

Toast position:

```
bottom center
```

Duration:

```
2 seconds
```

---

# Performance UX Rules

UI must remain fast.

Rules:

- lazy load images
- virtualize lists if >100 items
- avoid heavy renders

Target:

```
UI interaction <100ms
```

---

# AI Generated UI Code Rules

When AI agents generate UI:

They must:

- follow spacing system
- use component token architecture
- avoid introducing new design systems

Do not introduce:

```
Tailwind
Material UI
Chakra
```

Keep UI **simple and native-feeling**.

---

# Component Styling Rules

Components must follow this pattern:

1. Define component tokens inside the component root CSS
2. Map them to semantic tokens or spacing/radius primitives
3. Use component tokens internally
4. Keep all component styles in the component folder

Example:

```css
.card {
  --card-bg: var(--color-panel);
  --card-border: var(--color-border);
  --card-radius: var(--radius-2);
  --card-padding: var(--space-4);

  background: var(--card-bg);
  border: 1px solid var(--card-border);
  padding: var(--card-padding);
  border-radius: var(--card-radius);
}
```

---

# Hard Rules

Do NOT:

- hardcode colors
- hardcode spacing
- reference primitive tokens inside component style rules
- reference semantic tokens directly inside deeply nested elements
- create global styles for component-specific layout

Always:

- create component tokens for each component
- map component tokens to semantic or spacing tokens
- keep component styles in the component folder

---

# Benefits

This architecture provides:

- theme flexibility
- safe refactoring
- scalable component styling
- consistent UI patterns
- clear separation of concerns

All new UI code must follow this system.

